/**
\page org_mitk_views_mxnlayouteditor The MxN Layout Editor

\imageMacro{QmitkMxNMultiWidgetEditor.svg,"Icon of the MxN Layout Editor",16.00}

\tableofcontents

\section org_mitk_views_mxnlayouteditor_Overview Overview
	The MxN Layout Editor is the authoritative view of the whole layout document of an
	MxN display (\ref org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget): the window arrangement and all
	synchronization groups. Open it with the display's layout button, by clicking the
	sync barcode in any window's utility menu, or via <i>Window &rarr; Show View</i>.
	As a dockable view it stays open during review sessions and doubles as a live
	legend of the current coupling.

	The window map is the permanent canvas. Below it, the synchronization side has two
	faces on their own tabs: <b>Sync groups</b>, the group cards used for everyday
	work, and <b>Advanced</b>, the per-window link matrix. Only one is up at a time.

\section org_mitk_views_mxnlayouteditor_Layout The layout row
	The <b>Layout</b> row at the top of the view acts on the layout document as a whole:
	<ul>
		<li> <b>Presets</b> applies one of the arrangements that ship with MITK.
		<li> <b>Load...</b> replaces the current layout with one read from a layout file
			(see \ref org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_LayoutFile).
		<li> <b>Save...</b> writes the current layout to a file. A saved layout carries the
			synchronization groups as well as the window arrangement, so this is also how
			a change made purely on the synchronization side is kept.
	</ul>

	Applying a preset or a file replaces the window arrangement and discards the
	current synchronization groups, so the view asks for confirmation first unless the
	layout is still the trivial default.

	The grid of the arrangement is edited below the window map: the row and column
	buttons add or remove a trailing row or column in place, which leaves the existing
	windows and their settings untouched, while <b>Edit grid...</b> opens the picker for
	a grid of a chosen size or for an arrangement derived from the loaded data.

\section org_mitk_views_mxnlayouteditor_Map The window map
	The window map mirrors the current layout: one tile per render window, carrying a
	bar in its navigation group's color along its top edge (no bar marks windows whose
	navigation dimensions belong to different groups). Selected tiles are tinted, which
	keeps the tile edges free to carry group colors.

	Each tile shows the window's <b>sync barcode</b>: one glyph per dimension in a fixed
	order (pan, zoom, slice, crosshair, orientation, windowing, LUT) plus the data
	selection, tinted with the linked group's hue and left as a gap where the window is
	not synchronized on that dimension. An axis the window sits at an offset on has its
	box's upper-right corner filled in; only slice, zoom and pan can be offset, so the
	mark stays rare. The sync peek writes the value itself, which a tile has no room
	for. On a narrow tile the eight axes wrap into an even grid rather than shrink, so
	the glyphs stay as large as the tile allows. Hovering one of the glyphs lights up every window that shares that
	synchronization, which makes the coupling legible at a glance - and, while the
	<b>Advanced</b> tab is up, marks the same windows on its matrix. Hovering a chip
	there does the same in reverse, so the two surfaces answer as one.

\subsection org_mitk_views_mxnlayouteditor_Selection Selecting windows
	Selection follows the file-explorer model: a click selects the window clicked,
	Ctrl-click toggles a single window, and Shift-click takes the range from the window
	clicked last (Ctrl+Shift adds that range to the selection). A click on empty space
	clears the selection. Selecting a window in the map also makes it the active one in
	the display.

\subsection org_mitk_views_mxnlayouteditor_Assign Assigning windows to a group
	Selected windows join a group by clicking the group card's "Assign selection"
	button, by dragging the tiles onto the card, or by dragging the group's color swatch
	from the card onto a tile. "Remove selection" takes them out of the group again.

	A drop can join in three ways. A plain drop <b>replaces</b>: the window leaves its
	other groups and wholly joins the target. Holding <i>Alt</i> <b>merges</b>, keeping
	the window's links on dimensions the target group does not cover. Holding
	<i>Shift</i> <b>fills</b> only the dimensions on which the window is not yet
	synchronized. Dragging with the right mouse button instead asks for the mode in a
	menu when the drop happens.

	A window that joins a group which does not synchronize anything yet gets the
	navigation bundle (pan, zoom, slice, crosshair) as the common-case default.

\section org_mitk_views_mxnlayouteditor_Groups Group cards
	Each group card offers:
	<ul>
		<li> The color swatch sets the group's hue - the color that identifies the group
			on all synchronization surfaces. It is saved with the layout.
		<li> The name field sets the group's display name, shown identically everywhere.
		<li> The card's own sync barcode shows which dimensions the group synchronizes.
			Clicking an axis links or unlinks it for every window in the group; an axis
			drawn dashed is linked for only some of them, and one with a filled upper
			corner has at least one member sitting at an offset - the card speaks for
			several windows, so it marks that a shift exists without saying whose. For
			a group that has no windows yet, select windows in the map first and then
			click an axis to add them.
		<li> The dimension checkboxes control the same links for all member windows;
			"Link navigation" applies the pan, zoom, slice and crosshair bundle explicitly.
		<li> "Re-converge" re-establishes reference + offset for the group's
			slice/zoom/pan members after drift (e.g. from boundary clamping).
		<li> "Reinit geometry" re-initializes the shared reference geometry of the
			group's slice/orientation neighborhood; only these windows are affected,
			unlike the application-wide reinit of the Data Manager.
	</ul>

\section org_mitk_views_mxnlayouteditor_Advanced The advanced matrix
	The <b>Advanced</b> tab is the power-user face: every window as a row, every
	dimension as a column. Each cell shows the group the window links on that dimension
	as a chip in the group's color, carrying the group's name and, where one was
	authored, the offset. An empty cell is a window that is not synchronized on that
	dimension. The data-selection column is never empty, because a window's selection
	always belongs to some group. A column widens to keep its chips whole, so names
	are only ever shortened by a width you set yourself - widen the view rather than
	read an abbreviation.

	This face exposes the complete layout-file link model, including couplings between
	windows that are not adjacent on screen, and it is the deliberate way to author a
	partial group state in which only some windows are linked on a dimension. Everyday
	assignment is expected to happen in the map instead.

\subsection org_mitk_views_mxnlayouteditor_AdvancedEditing Editing the matrix
	The matrix is read; the bar beneath it is where it is edited, so nothing ever covers
	the grid being worked on. Select cells - drag across the grid, <i>Ctrl</i>-click to
	add one, <i>Shift</i>-click for a range - and the bar names the selection and offers
	what applies to it.

	When the selection is on a single dimension the bar carries that axis's glyph beside
	its name, the same glyph the column header and the tiles use; a selection spanning
	several dimensions shows none, because no one glyph would name it.

	Selecting windows in the map above selects their rows here, and selecting cells here
	selects those windows in the map. Hovering a chip marks every window sharing that
	synchronization, here and on the map at once. Clicking an axis header takes that
	whole column, clicking a window header that whole row, so linking every window on
	one dimension, or unlinking one window entirely, is two gestures.

	<ul>
		<li> <b>Group</b> assigns every selected cell. The list holds the groups that
			exist; groups are created with <b>+ Group</b>, in the same corner as on the
			<b>Sync groups</b> tab. Choosing "(not linked)" unlinks the selection, and
			on the data-selection axis returns the windows to the default group. The
			same list opens as a popup on right-click or double-click for a single
			quick edit, and <i>Del</i> unlinks the selection.
		<li> <b>Offset</b> appears for a selection that sits wholly on slice, zoom or pan
			and whose cells are all linked - an offset is relative to the group's seed,
			so an unlinked cell has nothing for it to be relative to. This is how a
			detail view is kept twice as zoomed, or a window parked a fixed distance
			from its group. Where the selected cells carry different offsets the editor
			reads "multiple" rather than any one of them, and that marker cannot be
			applied: dial a real value to give the whole selection one.
		<li> <b>Ramp</b> spreads slice offsets over the selected cells in the order the
			windows are listed in the line above, which authors a movie-frame layout
			showing slices -1 / 0 / +1 in one step. The offsets it would write are
			previewed beside it, so the effect reads against that window list before it
			is applied. It needs a selection wholly in one group: offsets in different
			groups are measured from different seeds, so a series spread across them
			would mean nothing. Slice only, for the same reason in a different form -
			zoom composes multiplicatively rather than by steps, and a pan ramp has no
			unambiguous direction in two dimensions.
	</ul>

	A change applies as soon as it is made and both faces show it at once, so an
	assignment made on the cards is on the chips here without reopening the tab.

*/
